= Solution
{parent=a}

The radial coordinate in the <cylindrical coordinate system> is the <Euclidean distance> from the $z$-axis:
$$
\boxed{r=\sqrt{x^2+y^2}=\sqrt2}.
$$

= b
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

In <spherical coordinates>, the radial distance is $\sqrt{x^2+y^2+z^2}=\sqrt3$ and $\cos\theta=z/r$. Since the point lies below the equatorial <plane>, the polar angle exceeds $\pi/2$:
$$
\boxed{\theta=\arccos\left(-\frac1{\sqrt3}\right)}.
$$
The angle is approximately $2.1863$ radians, or $125.26^\circ$.

= 3
{parent=Paper 2}
