= a
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For the <natural logarithm>, $f'(x)=1/x$. The chord <slope> is $(\log2-\log1)/(2-1)=\log2$. Hence the <mean value theorem> point satisfies $1/\xi=\log2$, giving
$$
\boxed{\xi=\frac1{\log2}}.
$$
It lies strictly between one and two. For example, $\log2=\int_1^2du/u$ lies strictly between $1/2$ and one.

= b
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The chord through the endpoints is the <straight line> $y=(x-1)\log2$. At $\xi=1/\log2$, the <tangent line> is
$$
y=\log\xi+\frac{x-\xi}{\xi}
=\log\xi+(x-\xi)\log2.
$$
These two <straight lines> have the same <slope>, as required by the <mean value theorem>. Since $f''(x)=-1/x^2<0$, the <natural logarithm> is a <concave function>: its graph lies above the chord and below this <tangent line>.

\Image[paper-2-q3-mean-value.png]
{title=The mean value theorem for the logarithm on the interval from one to two}
{description=The chord and the <tangent line> at $\xi=1/\log2$ are parallel. The marked point lies on the graph of the <natural logarithm>.}

