The <fundamental theorem of calculus> applies because the <integrand> is <continuous>. Thus
$$
\boxed{\frac{dI}{dx}=f(x)}.
$$
The requested <derivative> is $dI/dx$, as shown in the PDF; the local OCR corrupts its notation.

= b
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Use the <product rule> and the <fundamental theorem of calculus>:
$$
\frac d{dx}\left[\sin x\int_0^x\sin y\,dy\right]
=\cos x\int_0^x\sin y\,dy+\sin^2x.
$$
Since the <integral> is $1-\cos x$, this simplifies to
$$
\boxed{\cos x-\cos(2x)}.
$$

= 5
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=5}

The <Newton root-finding iteration> replaces the graph by its <tangent line> at $x_0$ and uses that line's zero:
$$
0=f(x_0)+f'(x_0)(x_1-x_0),\qquad
x_1=x_0-\frac{f(x_0)}{f'(x_0)}.
$$
Comparing with the printed reciprocal-ratio form gives
$$
\boxed{\alpha=-1,\qquad\beta=-1}.
$$
This step requires $f'(x_0)\ne0$. If $f(x_0)=0$, the iteration has already reached a <zero of a function> and the printed reciprocal-ratio expression need not be evaluated.

