{parent=9}

Expanding the <quadratic form> gives
$$
(x\ y)\begin{pmatrix}1&4\\a_{21}&a_{22}\end{pmatrix}\binom xy
=x^2+(4+a_{21})xy+a_{22}y^2.
$$
Matching <coefficients> of $xy$ and $y^2$ yields
$$
\boxed{a_{21}=4,\qquad a_{22}=4}.
$$

= 10
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=10}

The <trigonometric identity> gives $\sin x\cos x=\tfrac12\sin2x$, and the complementary-angle <trigonometric identity> gives $\cos(\pi/2-x)=\sin x$. Thus the <Fourier series> is already the finite expression
$$
\boxed{\sin x+\frac12\sin2x}.
$$
The only nonzero <Fourier coefficients> are $b_1=1$ and $b_2=1/2$.

= 11
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=11}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The scalar $\mathbf r\cdot\hat{\mathbf u}$ is the signed <scalar projection> along the <unit vector> $\hat{\mathbf u}$. Multiplying by that <unit vector> makes $P_{\hat{\mathbf u}}(\mathbf r)$ the <orthogonal projection> of $\mathbf r$ onto its span.

For the other expression, the <vector triple product identity> gives
$$
\boxed{R_{\hat{\mathbf u}}(\mathbf r)
=\mathbf r(\hat{\mathbf u}\cdot\hat{\mathbf u})
-\hat{\mathbf u}(\hat{\mathbf u}\cdot\mathbf r)
=\mathbf r-\hat{\mathbf u}(\mathbf r\cdot\hat{\mathbf u})}.
$$
This is the <orthogonal projection onto a plane> perpendicular to $\hat{\mathbf u}$ through the origin. Its <dot product> with $\hat{\mathbf u}$ is zero, while the difference from $\mathbf r$ is parallel to $\hat{\mathbf u}$.

= b
{parent=11}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The two <orthogonal projections> are complementary, giving
$$
\boxed{P_{\hat{\mathbf u}}(\mathbf r)+R_{\hat{\mathbf u}}(\mathbf r)=\mathbf r}.
$$
They split a <vector> into its components parallel and perpendicular to $\hat{\mathbf u}$.

= c
{parent=11}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The <straight line> consists of $\mathbf y+s(\mathbf z-\mathbf y)$, with $s\in\mathbb R$. Since the endpoints are distinct, the direction <vector> can be normalized. Choose
$$
\boxed{\mathbf p=\mathbf y,\qquad
\hat{\mathbf q}=\frac{\mathbf z-\mathbf y}{|\mathbf z-\mathbf y|}}.
$$
Putting $t=s|\mathbf z-\mathbf y|$ gives the required <parametric equation of a straight line> $\mathbf r=\mathbf p+t\hat{\mathbf q}$. Other choices of $\mathbf p$ on the same <straight line>, or the opposite direction for $\hat{\mathbf q}$, give equivalent descriptions.

= d
{parent=11}
{scope}

= i
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Take the <cross product> with $\hat{\mathbf q}$:
$$
\mathbf r\times\hat{\mathbf q}
=\mathbf p\times\hat{\mathbf q}
+t(\hat{\mathbf q}\times\hat{\mathbf q})
=\mathbf p\times\hat{\mathbf q}.
$$
Thus one choice is
$$
\boxed{\hat{\mathbf a}=\hat{\mathbf q},\qquad\mathbf c=\mathbf p\times\hat{\mathbf q}}.
$$
Conversely, this <cross product> equation implies $(\mathbf r-\mathbf p)\times\hat{\mathbf q}=0$, so $\mathbf r-\mathbf p$ is parallel to $\hat{\mathbf q}$. It therefore describes precisely the original <straight line>.

= ii
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The <unit vector> $\hat{\mathbf a}$ specifies the direction of the <straight line>. Reversing its sign, together with the sign of $\mathbf c$, leaves the same <straight line>.

= iii
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Decompose $\mathbf p$ into its <orthogonal projections> parallel and perpendicular to $\hat{\mathbf a}$. Since $\mathbf p\times\hat{\mathbf a}$ removes the parallel component,
$$
|\mathbf c|=|\mathbf p\times\hat{\mathbf a}|
=|\mathbf p-(\mathbf p\cdot\hat{\mathbf a})\hat{\mathbf a}|.
$$
The last <vector> is the displacement to the point of the <straight line> nearest the origin. Hence
$$
\boxed{c\text{ is the perpendicular distance from the origin to the line}}.
$$
The original PDF has $c=|\mathbf c|$, not the squared magnitude inserted by the OCR.

= iv
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

The <vector> $\mathbf c=\mathbf p\times\hat{\mathbf a}$ is a <normal vector> to the span of $\mathbf p$ and $\hat{\mathbf a}$. Since $\mathbf c\ne0$, these <vectors> determine a <plane>. Thus the missing words are

**the line and the origin**.

Indeed this <plane> consists of all <linear combinations> of $\mathbf p$ and the line's direction <vector>.

= e
{parent=11}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

A necessary compatibility condition is $\mathbf f\cdot\hat{\mathbf g}=0$, because a <cross product> with $\hat{\mathbf g}$ is perpendicular to it. If this condition fails, there is no solution.

If it holds, take $\hat{\mathbf g}\times$ the equation and use the <vector triple product identity>:
$$
\hat{\mathbf g}\times\mathbf f
=\hat{\mathbf g}\times(\mathbf r\times\hat{\mathbf g})
=\mathbf r-(\mathbf r\cdot\hat{\mathbf g})\hat{\mathbf g}.
$$
Thus the perpendicular <orthogonal projection> is fixed and the parallel component is free:
$$
\boxed{\mathbf r=\hat{\mathbf g}\times\mathbf f+s\hat{\mathbf g},\quad s\in\mathbb R,
\qquad\text{provided }\mathbf f\cdot\hat{\mathbf g}=0}.
$$
Conversely, $(\hat{\mathbf g}\times\mathbf f)\times\hat{\mathbf g}=\mathbf f$ under the compatibility condition, so every displayed <vector> solves the equation. The solution set is a <straight line>, including the line through the origin when $\mathbf f=0$.

= 12
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=12}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Differentiate with respect to the two <Cartesian coordinates>:
$$
\boxed{\nabla h=(6x^5-48xy^2,\ 6y^5-48x^2y)^T
=6\bigl(x(x^4-8y^2),\ y(y^4-8x^2)\bigr)^T}.
$$

= b
{parent=12}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <gradient> is horizontal when $h_y=0$ and vertical when $h_x=0$. Factoring those <partial derivatives> gives the two <loci>:
$$
\boxed{h_y=0:\quad y=0\quad\text{or}\quad x=\pm\frac{y^2}{2\sqrt2}},
$$
$$
\boxed{h_x=0:\quad x=0\quad\text{or}\quad y=\pm\frac{x^2}{2\sqrt2}}.
$$
Thus each <locus> consists of an axis and two <parabolas>. Their common points are the five <stationary points> found below. At those points the <gradient> is zero and has no direction, so they are included in both algebraic zero-component conditions but excluded if “parallel” is required to mean a nonzero directed <vector>.

\Image[paper-2-q12-loci.png]
{title=Loci of horizontal and vertical gradients}
{description=Blue curves have $h_y=0$, orange curves have $h_x=0$, and black points mark their intersections. At the black points the <gradient> itself vanishes.}

= c
{parent=12}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

A <stationary point> must satisfy both factored equations from part (a). If either coordinate is zero, the other equation forces the other coordinate to vanish. This gives $(0,0)$.

Otherwise $x^4=8y^2$ and $y^4=8x^2$. With $u=x^2>0$ and $v=y^2>0$, these become $u^2=8v$ and $v^2=8u$. Dividing gives $(u/v)^3=1$, so $u=v$. Then $u^2=8u$ forces $u=v=8$. Hence all five <stationary points> are
$$
\boxed{(0,0),\qquad(2\sqrt2\,\varepsilon,\ 2\sqrt2\,\delta),\quad \varepsilon,\delta\in\{-1,1\}}.
$$

= d
{parent=12}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

The <Hessian matrix> is
$$
H=\begin{pmatrix}30x^4-48y^2&-96xy\\-96xy&30y^4-48x^2\end{pmatrix}.
$$
At each nonzero <stationary point>, its diagonal entries are $1536$ and the off-diagonal entries are $\pm768$. Its <eigenvalues> are $768$ and $2304$, both positive. Thus the <Hessian matrix> is <positive-definite matrix>[positive definite], and the <second-derivative test> proves that
$$
\boxed{\text{all four nonzero stationary points are strict local minima}}.
$$
Their common value is $h=-512$. In fact they are <global minima>: with $u=|xy|$, the <arithmetic-geometric mean inequality> gives $x^6+y^6\geq2u^3$, so
$$
h\geq2u^3-24u^2\geq-512.
$$
The last one-variable <polynomial> attains its minimum at $u=8$, and equality in the first bound requires $|x|=|y|$, precisely the four points above.

= e
{parent=12}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

Along either coordinate axis, the restriction is $h(t,0)=h(0,t)=t^6$, which is positive for $t\ne0$. Along either diagonal it is
$$
h(t,t)=h(t,-t)=2t^6-24t^4=2t^4(t^2-12),
$$
which is negative for sufficiently small nonzero $t$. Therefore every neighbourhood of the origin contains both positive and negative values, whereas $h(0,0)=0$. Consequently
$$
\boxed{(0,0)\text{ is a saddle point, neither a local minimum nor a local maximum}}.
$$
The <Hessian matrix> vanishes there, so the ordinary <second-derivative test> is inconclusive; the restrictions settle the question.

\Image[paper-2-q12-origin-sections.png]
{title=Opposite signs along axes and diagonals establish the saddle at the origin}
{description=The two axes give $t^6>0$, while the two diagonals give $2t^6-24t^4<0$ near zero. The panels use different vertical scales to make both signs visible.}

= f
{parent=12}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=f}

Near a nonzero <stationary point> $\mathbf p$, <Taylor expansion> of the <gradient> gives $\nabla h(\mathbf p+\mathbf v)=H(\mathbf p)\mathbf v+O(|\mathbf v|^2)$. Since the <Hessian matrix> is <positive-definite matrix>[positive definite], $\mathbf v\cdot\nabla h(\mathbf p+\mathbf v)>0$ for sufficiently small nonzero $\mathbf v$. Thus the <gradient> arrows point away from each of the four <local minima>, with different strengths in the two <eigenvector> directions.

At the origin, on the $x$-axis the <gradient> is $(6x^5,0)$ and on the $y$-axis it is $(0,6y^5)$, so arrows point outward. On $y=x$ the <gradient> is $6x^3(x^2-8)(1,1)$, and on $y=-x$ it is $6x^3(x^2-8)(1,-1)$, so arrows point toward the origin for small $|x|$. Away from the axes the leading terms are $(-48xy^2,-48x^2y)$, pointing inward in both coordinate signs. The narrow outward sectors close to the axes are visible in the exact <vector field>.

\Image[paper-2-q12-gradients.png]
{title=Gradient directions near all five stationary points}
{description=Arrows show normalized <gradient> directions, not the direction of descent. They point outward near the four minima, while the degenerate origin has outward axis directions and inward diagonal directions.}

= 13
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=13}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For $\mathbf G$, the <product rule> for the <curl> gives
$$
\nabla\times\mathbf G
=\nabla(\mathbf a\cdot\mathbf r)\times\mathbf r
+(\mathbf a\cdot\mathbf r)(\nabla\times\mathbf r)
=\mathbf a\times\mathbf r.
$$
Since $\mathbf a\ne0$, this is not identically zero on $\mathbb R^3$. A <gradient field> has zero <curl>, so **no scalar potential exists for $\mathbf G$ on the whole space**.

For the radial <vector field> $\mathbf K$, observe that
$$
\nabla\left[\frac12\log(1+x^2+y^2+z^2)\right]
=\frac{(x,y,z)^T}{1+x^2+y^2+z^2}=\mathbf K.
$$
Thus all <vector-field potentials> are
$$
\boxed{\phi(\mathbf r)=\frac12\log(1+|\mathbf r|^2)+C}.
$$
These are <smooth> everywhere, including the origin. Any two differ by a constant because their <gradient> difference vanishes on the <connected> whole space.

= b
{parent=13}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Parameterize the <straight line> by $\mathbf r(t)=(t,t,t)^T$, $0\leq t\leq1$. Then $\mathbf r'=(1,1,1)^T$ and the <line integral> becomes
$$
\int_0^1\left[2t^2+(\mu+1)t^2+(\lambda+1)t^2\right]dt
=\boxed{\frac{\lambda+\mu+4}{3}}.
$$

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

On the circular arc, $z=0$ and $dz=0$, so the <line integral> reduces to
$$
\int(x^2\,dx+y^2\,dy)
=\left[\frac{x^3+y^3}{3}\right]_{(0,0)}^{(1,1)}=\frac23.
$$
This <exact differential> evaluation applies to the specified arc; for example its <parametric equation> is $(x,y,z)=(1-\cos\theta,\sin\theta,0)$, $0\leq\theta\leq\pi/2$. On the vertical segment $x=y=1$, only $dz$ contributes, giving $\int_0^1(\lambda+1)\,dz=\lambda+1$. The whole path therefore gives
$$
\boxed{\lambda+\frac53}.
$$
To answer the final conservativeness requirement, compute the <curl>:
$$
\nabla\times\mathbf M=((2-\mu)y,\ (1-2\lambda)x,\ 0)^T.
$$
It vanishes identically exactly when
$$
\boxed{\mu=2,\qquad\lambda=\frac12}.
$$
On the <simply connected> whole space this condition is sufficient for a <conservative vector field>. Explicitly a <vector-field potential> is
$$
\phi=\frac12x^2z+y^2z+\frac{x^3+y^3}{3}+C.
$$
Both path calculations then give $13/6$, equal to $\phi(1,1,1)-\phi(0,0,0)$ by the <fundamental theorem for line integrals>. Equality of just these two path values would only force $\mu=2\lambda+1$; it is the <curl> calculation that establishes full <path independence>.

= 14
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=14}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

A displayed minute contains one minute of possible start times, so the specified <uniform distribution> has <probability density function>
$$
\boxed{p(s)=\begin{cases}1,&0\leq s<1,\\0,&\text{otherwise}.\end{cases}}
$$
Endpoint choices for a <probability density function> do not affect any <probabilities>.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The graph is a rectangle of height one over $[0,1)$, with zero height elsewhere. Its unit <area> is the required normalization of the <probability density function>.

\Image[paper-2-q14-timing-densities.png]
{title=Uniform endpoint uncertainty and triangular elapsed-time uncertainty}
{description=Left: the <uniform distribution> of one endpoint offset. Right: the <triangular distribution> of the difference of two independent endpoint offsets. Shading marks the event $T>1/2$.}

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

For the <uniform distribution>, the <expected value> and second <moment> are
$$
\mathbb E[S]=\int_0^1s\,ds=\frac12,\qquad
\mathbb E[S^2]=\int_0^1s^2\,ds=\frac13.
$$
Hence the <variance> is
$$
\boxed{\mathbb E[S]=\frac12\text{ minute},\qquad
\operatorname{Var}(S)=\frac13-\frac14=\frac1{12}\text{ minute}^2}.
$$

= b
{parent=14}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Let $E$ be the finish-time offset from the start of its displayed minute. The elapsed time is $25+E-S$, so $T=E-S$. To obtain the stated density, use the intended model that $E$ and $S$ are <independent random variables>, both with the <uniform distribution> on $[0,1)$.

Their joint <probability density function> is one on the unit square. Equivalently, the <convolution of probability densities> gives
$$
q(t)=\int_{\mathbb R}p(s)p(s+t)\,ds.
$$
The <integrand> is one exactly where $s\in[0,1]$ and $s+t\in[0,1]$. The overlap has length $1+t$ for $-1\leq t<0$, length $1-t$ for $0\leq t<1$, and zero otherwise. Therefore
$$
\boxed{q(t)=\begin{cases}1-|t|,&|t|<1,\\0,&|t|\geq1.\end{cases}}
$$
This is a symmetric <triangular distribution>. The independence and finish-time uniformity are additional modelling assumptions: the two truncated clock readings alone do not imply them. For example, if $E=S$ then the elapsed time is exactly twenty-five minutes and $T=0$, even though each endpoint offset is individually uniform.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The <probability density function> graph joins $(-1,0)$ to $(0,1)$ to $(1,0)$ and is zero elsewhere, as shown in the right panel of the timing figure. Its <area> is
$$
\int_{-\infty}^\infty q(t)\,dt
=2\int_0^1(1-t)\,dt=\boxed{1}.
$$
Thus the <probability distribution> is normalized.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Since $q$ is an <even function>, $tq(t)$ is an <odd function> and its <integral> over the symmetric interval vanishes. Thus $\mathbb E[T]=0$. Its <variance> is
$$
\mathbb E[T^2]=2\int_0^1t^2(1-t)\,dt
=2\left(\frac13-\frac14\right)=\frac16.
$$
Consequently
$$
\boxed{\mathbb E[T]=0,\qquad\operatorname{SD}(T)=\frac1{\sqrt6}\text{ minute}}.
$$
The <standard deviation> is approximately $0.40825$ minute, or $24.49$ seconds.

= iv
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

An excess of thirty seconds is $T>1/2$ in minute units. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P(T>1/2)=\int_{1/2}^1(1-t)\,dt=\frac18}.
$$
This is the shaded triangular <area> under the <probability density function>.

= c
{parent=14}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The <random variable> $T$ is the sum $E+(-S)$. The <linearity of expectation> gives
$$
\mathbb E[T]=\mathbb E[E]-\mathbb E[S]=\frac12-\frac12=0.
$$
Multiplication by $-1$ reverses the <mean> but preserves the <variance>. Under the independence assumption used in part (b), the <variance of a sum> gives
$$
\operatorname{Var}(T)=\operatorname{Var}(E)+\operatorname{Var}(-S)
=\frac1{12}+\frac1{12}=\frac16.
$$
Thus both predictions agree with the direct <integrals>. For the actual elapsed time, adding twenty-five changes the <mean> to twenty-five minutes and leaves the <variance> at $1/6$ minute squared.

Without independence, the correct expression is $\operatorname{Var}(T)=1/6-2\operatorname{Cov}(E,S)$ when both marginals are uniform. This explains why independent endpoint errors must be part of the intended model rather than a consequence of the display format.

= 15
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=15}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The <characteristic equation> has the repeated <polynomial root> $m=-2$, because both $e^{-2x}$ and $xe^{-2x}$ occur as independent solutions. Hence its <characteristic polynomial> is
$$
(m+2)^2=m^2+4m+4.
$$
Comparing constant terms gives
$$
\boxed{\gamma=4}.
$$

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The <characteristic equation> factors as
$$
m^2+6m+5=(m+1)(m+5)=0.
$$
The distinct <characteristic roots> therefore give the <general solution>
$$
\boxed{y(x)=C_1e^{-x}+C_2e^{-5x}}.
$$

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The <characteristic roots> are
$$
m=\frac{-3\pm\sqrt{9-16}}2=-\frac32\pm i\frac{\sqrt7}{2}.
$$
Taking real <linear combinations> of the two <complex exponential> solutions yields
$$
\boxed{y(x)=e^{-3x/2}\left[C_1\cos\left(\frac{\sqrt7}{2}x\right)
+C_2\sin\left(\frac{\sqrt7}{2}x\right)\right]}.
$$
The coefficient $\beta$ is three here, as confirmed in the PDF; the OCR replaces it by an undefined symbol.

