{parent=a}

Use the real <inner product>
$$
\langle f,g\rangle=\int_{-L}^{L}f(x)g(x)\,dx.
$$
<Orthogonality> means this <integral> is zero for any two distinct listed <functions>. The individual squared <norms> are positive: the constant function has squared <norm> $2L$, while every listed <sine> or <cosine> has squared <norm> $L$. Thus they are orthogonal but not normalized to unit length in this <inner product>.

= b
{parent=18}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Take the <inner product> of the <Fourier series> with $\sin(n\pi x/L)$. <Fourier orthogonality> eliminates the constant, all cosine terms, and all sine terms except the $n$th. The remaining squared <norm> is
$$
\int_{-L}^{L}\sin^2(n\pi x/L)\,dx=L,
$$
so
$$
\boxed{b_n=\frac1L\int_{-L}^{L}f(x)\sin(n\pi x/L)\,dx,\qquad n\geq1}.
$$
For a <square-integrable function>, the calculation is justified by taking <inner products> of finite <Fourier partial sums> and passing to their <convergence in L2>[mean-square limit].

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The analogous <orthogonal projection> onto the cosine terms gives
$$
\boxed{a_n=\frac1L\int_{-L}^{L}f(x)\cos(n\pi x/L)\,dx,\qquad n\geq0}.
$$
For $n=0$, this means $a_0=L^{-1}\int_{-L}^{L}f(x)\,dx$; the actual constant in the <Fourier series> is $a_0/2$ because the constant function has squared <norm> $2L$.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Let $S_N$ be the <Fourier partial sum> through frequency $N$. By <orthogonality> and the coefficient formulas,
$$
\int_{-L}^{L}S_N^2\,dx
=\frac{La_0^2}{2}+L\sum_{n=1}^{N}(a_n^2+b_n^2),
\qquad
\int_{-L}^{L}fS_N\,dx=\int_{-L}^{L}S_N^2\,dx.
$$
Expanding the nonnegative squared <L2 norm> of the error gives the exact finite identity
$$
\int_{-L}^{L}(f-S_N)^2\,dx
=\int_{-L}^{L}f^2\,dx-\frac{La_0^2}{2}-L\sum_{n=1}^{N}(a_n^2+b_n^2).
$$
For $f$ in the <L2 space>, completeness of the <Fourier basis> makes the left-hand side tend to zero. Dividing the resulting equality by $2L$ proves <Parseval's identity>:
$$
\boxed{\frac1{2L}\int_{-L}^{L}f(x)^2\,dx
=\frac{a_0^2}{4}+\frac12\sum_{n=1}^\infty(a_n^2+b_n^2)}.
$$
This argument uses <convergence in L2> rather than an unqualified exchange of an infinite sum with the <integral> of its square. The <function> in part (c) is continuous and piecewise polynomial, so these hypotheses hold.

