= Solution
{parent=a}

A central cross-section of the <sphere> gives the constraint $r^2+h^2/4=R^2$, and the cylinder's <volume> is $V=\pi r^2h$. At an interior optimum with $r,h>0$, the <Lagrange multiplier> equations for this <constrained optimization> problem are
$$
2\pi rh=2\lambda r,\qquad
\pi r^2=\lambda h/2,\qquad
r^2+h^2/4=R^2.
$$
The first gives $\lambda=\pi h$; substitution into the second gives $r^2=h^2/2$. The constraint then gives
$$
\boxed{h=\frac{2R}{\sqrt3},\qquad r=R\sqrt{\frac23}}.
$$
The feasible nonnegative $(r,h)$ form a compact arc, whose endpoints have zero <volume>. Since this is the only positive stationary candidate, it is the <global maximum>. Its <volume> and fraction of the sphere's <volume> are
$$
\boxed{V_{\max}=\frac{4\pi R^3}{3\sqrt3},\qquad
\frac{V_{\max}}{V_{\mathrm{sphere}}}=\frac1{\sqrt3}}.
$$

= b
{parent=19}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Use the <Lagrange multiplier> condition $\nabla f=\lambda\nabla g$ for $g=4x^2+y^2/16$:
$$
y=8\lambda x,\qquad x=\lambda y/8,\qquad4x^2+y^2/16=1.
$$
Neither coordinate can vanish at a constrained <stationary point>: either would force both to vanish, contradicting the constraint. Substitution therefore gives $\lambda^2=1$, so $y=8x$ or $y=-8x$. In either case $8x^2=1$, giving
$$
\boxed{(x,y)=\left(\frac{\varepsilon}{2\sqrt2},\ 2\sqrt2\,\delta\right),\qquad
\varepsilon,\delta\in\{-1,1\},\qquad f=\varepsilon\delta}.
$$
Equal signs give the two points attaining the <global maximum> $f=1$; opposite signs give the two <global minima> with $f=-1$. These exhaust the extrema because the <ellipse> is compact, its constraint <gradient> never vanishes on it, and $f$ is smooth. The bound can also be checked by setting $X=2x$, $Y=y/4$: then $X^2+Y^2=1$ and $f=2XY$, so $|f|\leq1$.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For a point in the first quadrant, $xy$ is the <area> of the axis-aligned rectangle with opposite vertices at the origin and $(x,y)$. Thus the maximum says that the largest such quarter-rectangle with its outer corner on the <ellipse> has <area> one.

Equivalently, reflect this rectangle in both axes. Its four vertices $(\pm|x|,\pm|y|)$ lie on the <ellipse>, and its full <area> is $4|xy|$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\text{the maximum area of an axis-aligned rectangle inscribed in the ellipse is }4}.
$$
Its width is $1/\sqrt2$ and its height is $4\sqrt2$.

