= a
{parent=20}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The <wave equation> is a homogeneous <linear partial differential equation>. Consequently, if $\psi_1$ and $\psi_2$ solve it, every constant <linear combination> $A\psi_1+B\psi_2$ also solves it. This is the <superposition principle>, because <partial differentiation> distributes over the <linear combination>.

The same statement holds for the homogeneous <Neumann boundary conditions>. <Initial conditions> combine in the same way; an arbitrary combination need not retain a fixed nonzero initial displacement.

= b
{parent=20}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The equation as printed requires $v\ne0$; its sign does not affect the result. Seek separated solutions $\psi=X(x)T(t)$. Substitution into the <wave equation> gives
$$
\frac{X''}{X}=\frac{T''}{v^2T}=-\lambda,
\qquad X''+\lambda X=0,\qquad T''+v^2\lambda T=0.
$$
The <Neumann boundary conditions> impose $X'(0)=X'(\pi)=0$. Multiplying the spatial equation by $X$ and <integration by parts> gives $\int_0^\pi(X')^2dx=\lambda\int_0^\pi X^2dx$, so nontrivial spatial <eigenfunctions> require $\lambda\geq0$.

For $\lambda=0$, $X$ is constant and $T=A_0+B_0t$. For $\lambda=k^2>0$, $X=C\cos kx+D\sin kx$. The first <boundary condition> gives $D=0$, and the second gives $\sin k\pi=0$. Thus $k=n$ is a positive integer and the spatial <eigenfunctions> are $\cos nx$. Their time dependence is $A_n\cos(nvt)+B_n\sin(nvt)$. By <superposition>,
$$
\psi=A_0+B_0t+\sum_{n\geq1}\bigl[A_n\cos(nvt)+B_n\sin(nvt)\bigr]\cos nx.
$$
The zero initial <velocity> sets $B_0=B_n=0$. The <trigonometric identity> gives the initial profile $\cos^2(x/2)=(1+\cos x)/2$, so only the constant mode and the first cosine mode occur. Hence
$$
\boxed{\psi(x,t)=\frac12+\frac12\cos x\cos(vt)}.
$$
Its spatial <derivative> is $-\tfrac12\sin x\cos(vt)$, which vanishes at both boundaries, and its time <derivative> vanishes initially. Thus it satisfies all the required data.

= c
{parent=20}
{scope}

= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

For the two travelling profiles, the <initial conditions> imply
$$
f(x)+g(x)=\cos^2(x/2),\qquad -vf'(x)+vg'(x)=0.
$$
Since $v\ne0$, $g-f$ is constant. One may therefore choose
$$
f(x)=\tfrac12\cos^2(x/2)+C,\qquad
g(x)=\tfrac12\cos^2(x/2)-C,
$$
where the constants cancel in the sum. The <D'Alembert formula> then gives
$$
\begin{aligned}
\psi(x,t)&=\frac12\cos^2\left(\frac{x-vt}{2}\right)
+\frac12\cos^2\left(\frac{x+vt}{2}\right)\\
&=\boxed{\frac12+\frac12\cos x\cos(vt)}.
\end{aligned}
$$
This agrees exactly with the restricted-domain solution from part (b). The whole-line cosine profile already has zero spatial <derivative> at both $x=0$ and $x=\pi$ for every time, so its restriction satisfies the <Neumann boundary conditions> without further modification.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The <standing wave> oscillates about the constant level $1/2$, with time period $2\pi/|v|$. Suitable snapshots are
$$
\begin{aligned}
t=0:&\quad\psi=\tfrac12(1+\cos x),\\
t=\frac{\pi}{3|v|}:&\quad\psi=\tfrac12+\tfrac14\cos x,\\
t=\frac{\pi}{2|v|}:&\quad\psi=\tfrac12,\\
t=\frac{\pi}{|v|}:&\quad\psi=\tfrac12(1-\cos x),\\
t=\frac{2\pi}{|v|}:&\quad\psi=\tfrac12(1+\cos x).
\end{aligned}
$$
The nonconstant amplitude shrinks to zero at a quarter-period, reverses sign at half a period, and returns after a full period. The <nodes of a standing wave> of the oscillatory part, $x=\pi/2+k\pi$, remain at height $1/2$.

\Image[paper-2-q20-standing-wave.png]
{title=Time evolution of the whole-line Neumann-compatible standing wave}
{description=Snapshots at phases $|v|t=0$, $\pi/3$, $\pi/2$ and $\pi$ illustrate contraction, flattening and reversal of the cosine profile. A full-period snapshot repeats the initial curve.}
